<div class="wrap">
  <header class="hero">
    <span class="eyebrow">Competitor Read · Brand Method</span>
    <h1>The field, <em>and the gap in it</em></h1>
    <p class="lede">Every solat app treats dua as filler. Every dua app treats solat as absent. The Malaysian market has strong utilities and strong content apps, and nothing that leads with the dua and carries utility-grade waktu solat behind it. That seam is the position.</p>
    <div class="vrow"><span class="vchip">7 apps read</span><span class="vchip">Malaysian market first</span><span class="vchip">Researched 5 Aug 2026</span></div>
  </header>

  <div class="execsum">
    <span class="eyebrow">The read, in 60 seconds</span>
    <h2 style="margin-top:12px;max-width:36ch">Utilities without a soul, content without a spine.</h2>
    <p class="sec-intro">The market splits clean. On one side sit the utilities: MySolat, iSolat, TheNoor, Waktu Solat Malaysia, all competing on the same JAKIM data with the same list layout, differentiated by ads or the absence of them. On the other side sit content apps: Muslim Pro's everything-store, QalbyApp's lifestyle layer, and a shelf of Hisnul Muslim clones with no design language at all. <strong>Nobody owns the dua as a designed object.</strong> Khayr's deck is the moat. The solat and qibla work is the price of entry it has now paid.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="chapdiv" id="c1"><div class="cd-no">01</div><div><div class="cd-h">The benchmark</div><div class="cd-sub">Seven apps against five questions</div></div></div>
  <section>
    <div class="tscroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>App</th><th>Times source</th><th>Qibla</th><th>Dua library</th><th>Model</th><th>What Khayr takes from it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="dim">Muslim Pro</td><td>Location-based, multiple calc methods, worldwide</td><td>Compass finder</td><td>Duas and dhikr with tasbih, plus full Quran</td><td>Freemium, heavy ads until paid</td><td class="take">The scale of the bundle, and the warning: ads and a 2020 data-sharing scandal cost it trust it never fully rebuilt. Khayr sells honesty instead.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="dim">TheNoor</td><td>JAKIM calculation, worldwide with auto and manual</td><td>Real-time location detection</td><td>Quran-first with translations and voice-over</td><td>Free, no ads, celebrity-backed</td><td class="take">Proof a Malaysian app can be free, clean and loved. Its dua layer is thin: the deck goes where it does not.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="dim">MySolat</td><td>Official JAKIM, auto location across Malaysia</td><td>Direction guidance</td><td>Daily remembrance, recitations</td><td>Free, no ads, no registration</td><td class="take">Auto-locating the zone. This is the one convenience Khayr still lacks, and it is on the plan.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="dim">iSolat</td><td>JAKIM released data, purist position</td><td>Basic</td><td>None to speak of</td><td>Free, academic origin</td><td class="take">Validates the audit's point: official data is the credibility line in Malaysia. Calculation alone marks an app as foreign.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="dim">QalbyApp</td><td>Prayer times with azan notifications</td><td>Compass</td><td>Duas by type, journal, lectures, articles</td><td>Free, lifestyle brand</td><td class="take">The emotional register. Its journal shows readers want a relationship with the content, which Khayr's notes and collections already serve.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="dim">Waktu Solat Malaysia</td><td>e-solat JAKIM, plus Brunei, Singapore, Indonesia</td><td>Magnetic compass that vibrates on alignment</td><td>Thin</td><td>Free with ads</td><td class="take">The vibrate-on-alignment detail, worth borrowing in the native port. Neighbour-country zones are a cheap widening later.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="dim">Athan · IslamicFinder</td><td>Worldwide calculation, azan alarms</td><td>Qibla finder</td><td>Dua and azkar with audio, tasbih, Islamic calendar</td><td>Freemium, ads</td><td class="take">Dua audio recitation. Khayr's Listen button already streams verse recitation: extending it to the full deck is a premium line competitors charge for.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <span class="pin"><b>The Subuh test:</b> ask one question of any app in this market: where does your Subuh time come from. Calculated apps sit roughly ten minutes off JAKIM's table. Khayr fetches the official number and falls back to calculation only when offline or abroad, which is the same order MySolat and iSolat compete on, now matched.</span>
  </section>

  <div class="chapdiv" id="c2"><div class="cd-no">02</div><div><div class="cd-h">The dua shelf</div><div class="cd-sub">The direct category, briefly</div></div></div>
  <section>
    <p class="sec-intro">Search any store for dua and the shelf is Hisnul Muslim collections: complete, authentic, and interchangeable. List layouts, ad banners, no sources shown per card, no design language. The category has content parity and zero product craft. <strong>Khayr's card anatomy, its source keys on every card, and its refusal to type Arabic by hand are all invisible to these competitors</strong> because none of them compete on integrity or on form.</p>
    <div class="grid g3">
      <div class="card"><span class="tag">They have</span><h3>Coverage</h3><p>Hundreds of duas per app, every occasion, every month. Khayr holds thirty. The content pipeline in the plan is how the gap closes without losing verification.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><span class="tag">They lack</span><h3>Provenance</h3><p>No card-level source keys, no review states, no byte-exact Arabic discipline. Khayr's integrity rules are a durable difference no clone can fake quickly.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><span class="tag">Nobody has</span><h3>The object</h3><p>A dua as a designed card you hold, flip, spread on a table and keep. The deck is the product. Everything else in the market is a list.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="chapdiv" id="c3"><div class="cd-no">03</div><div><div class="cd-h">Position</div><div class="cd-sub">One sentence, then the proof burden</div></div></div>
  <section>
    <p class="sec-intro"><em>The right dua for what you are facing, with the day's prayers built in.</em> The deck answers a moment, the times anchor a day, and the two together give Khayr a daily-open reason no dua shelf has and a soul no utility has. The proof burden is threefold: surface the times so they are glanceable, grow the library through the verified pipeline, and keep the premium honest while competitors sell vapour.</p>
